\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For binary strings of known length n and fixed rational retention probability $p>0$, can one uniform randomized multitape algorithm reconstruct every input string with success $\geq 2/3$ using $\exp (O_p((\log $ $n)^{3}(1+\log $ $\log (2n))^{6}))$ independent deletion traces and bit time polynomial in n, the binary length of p and the trace budget, on every trace stream and random-bit path?

\subsection{Short English statement}
For binary strings of known length n and fixed rational retention probability $p>0$, can one uniform randomized multitape algorithm reconstruct every input string with success $\geq 2/3$ using $\exp (O_p((\log $ $n)^{3}(1+\log $ $\log (2n))^{6}))$ independent deletion traces and bit time polynomial in n, the binary length of p and the trace budget, on every trace stream and random-bit path?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] Original question source or mathematical context.
\url{https://afonsobandeira.wordpress.com/2015/11/27/10l42grouptesting/}.
\item[S2] OpenAI, manuscript citation page. Source of the formulation motivating this local problem.
\url{https://github.com/openai/math/blob/adc7f1241b42e322a6451854ab7e4b4c146bf78a/preprints/Uniform-quasipolynomial-time-trace-reconstruction-October-5-2026/README.md}.
\end{itemize}

